a = \\arccos\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}2\\right)= \\dfrac\\pi 4\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle, as desired." 13194139535838 false] [17592186184055 1145 "The orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S} \\: \\mathbf{x} = A(A^T\\!A)^{-1}\\!A^T\\,\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070757/assets/9cdf7153834225217e3cfd680182777f47f2cc0d09185a66742b46fc67ebd7b6.png)\nTherefore, we get the following projection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}27 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 27\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{72}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $\\mathbf{x}$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\textrm{proj}_S \\mathbf{x}.$ Equivalently, it is the acute angle between $\\mathbf{x}$ and any non-zero vector that is parallel to $\\textrm{proj}_S \\mathbf{x}.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070757/assets/4e59afb848b5180340b05874c2dd63d04c22186c0c8e2235535e19551cab983a.png)\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{0\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{(-1)}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arccos\\left( \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}2\\right)= \\dfrac\\pi 4,\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle, as desired." 13194139535838 true] [17592186184116 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369287 13194139535838 true] [17592186184116 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369322 13194139535838 true] [17592186184116 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369350 13194139535838 true] [17592186184116 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369357 13194139535838 true] [17592186184117 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369315 13194139535838 true] [17592186184117 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369336 13194139535838 true] [17592186184117 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369343 13194139535838 true] [17592186184117 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369371 13194139535838 true] [17592186184148 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369294 13194139535838 true] [17592186184148 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369301 13194139535838 true] [17592186184148 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369308 13194139535838 true] [17592186184148 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369329 13194139535838 true] [17592186184148 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369364 13194139535838 true] [17592186184717 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535838 true] [17592186184717 1100 "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}],\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-9{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535838 true] [17592186184717 1100 "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$x = \\begi